><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251113130825+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="f3be6467-7d57-423c-af19-064de59abcf4" date="D:20251113130949+10'00'" name="a59c282c-e2dc-4397-bc55-3f76073bc8ba" icon="Comment" page="0" rect="84.320969,237.712158,108.320969,261.712158" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14b: there were some practical reflections on the outcomes of the study</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="637.179993,169.712158,821.179993,261.712158"
/></text
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251113130858+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="f3be6467-7d57-423c-af19-064de59abcf4" date="D:20251113130903+10'00'" name="3756374b-8e4d-4642-9597-aeee573ff018" icon="Comment" page="0" rect="84.320969,237.712158,108.320969,261.712158" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#007EAA;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>29</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In conclusion, results from this case study of a professional ath-lete recovering from acute myeloid leukaemia suggests that whilsttherapeutic venesections may still be required to manage high lev-els of serum ferritin (e.g. &gt;1000  g L</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>−1</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>), the addition of altitude </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>exposure may be a method to assist in minimizing the numberof venesections required to reduce iron stores. However, altitudetraining will not shift iron stores from the body, rather, as evi-denced in this case by the increase in transferrin at various timepoints along with rapid increase in reticulocyte percentage dur-ing the study period but not the control period, it appears altitudetraining may help mobilise the available (excessive) iron to incor-porate into haemoglobin. Furthermore, in this case, intermittentaltitude exposure in-season did not appear to affect performance.Thus, consistent with other work, it appears individuals with ele-vated iron availability are likely well placed to achieve increases inHb</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>mass</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>beyond what would be considered typical for those ironstores within ‘normal range’ (i.e. serum ferritin 35–100  g L</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>−1</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>),especially when exposed to altitude.</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#007FAB;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>18,19</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>However, it should bepointed out that performance improvements may have also been, inpart, a function of improved fatigue status associated with reducediron toxicity. Certainly, larger scale prospective studies would bebeneficial. Future studies may also wish to undertake follow upMRI.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="637.179993,169.712158,821.179993,261.712158"
/></text
